{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 20, "model_name": "meta/llama-4-maverick-17b-128e-instruct", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli was a nobleman and leader of the County of Tripoli a crusader state in the Holy Land. He was born around 1140 and died in 1187. Raymond was the son of Raymond II Count of Tripoli and Hodierna of Jerusalem. His mother was the sister of King Baldwin III of Jerusalem and King Amalric I of Jerusalem.\n\n*Early Life and Reign* Raymond III succeeded his father as Count of Tripoli at a young age after his father was assassinated in 1152. Due to his minority a regent was appointed to govern the county until he came of age. Raymond was taken to the court of King Baldwin III of Jerusalem where he was raised and educated. He was known to be a skilled military leader and a shrewd politician.\n\nAs Count of Tripoli Raymond III played a significant role in the politics of the crusader states. He was a member of the Haute Cour of Jerusalem and was involved in the decision-making process of the kingdom. *Military Campaigns and Conflicts* Raymond III led several military campaigns against the Zengid and Fatimid forces. He fought alongside King Amalric I of Jerusalem in the invasion of Egypt in 1164 and was captured by Nur ad-Din Zangi during the siege of Harim. He was released from captivity in 1174 after paying a ransom.\n\n*Succession Crisis and Death* Raymond III died in 1187 without an heir. He had previously adopted his godson Raymond of Antioch as his successor but later changed his mind and appointed his cousin Bohemond IV of Tripoli as his heir. However the succession was disputed and the county was eventually inherited by Bohemond IV. Raymond III's death marked the end of an era in the County of Tripoli and the crusader states. He was remembered as a wise and capable leader who played a significant role in the history of the Holy Land. Raymond III's legacy continued to shape the politics of the region for many years a